iconic location is  one that's no longer there the carlsbad  gap  it's a parking lot now but scroll back a  few years on google earth  and you'll see it there is a set of  stairs  that goes from one level to another at  the schoolyard and then next to it was a  patch of grass  and the grass went down at the same  angle as the stairs for the most part  but was actually a little bit less steep  and then there was just a wall  drop off at the end if you're jumping  the gap  you have to clear this higher part that  means you have to go much faster  and go actually much further if you  could do a trick down the stairs that's  one thing if you do a trick over the gap  that's a totally different thing  i've watched in re-watch skaters attempt  to land tricks at these spots over  and over and over and i realize that the  truly exhilarating moments happen only  after the skateboarder has failed a  hundred times  when chris called it a 360 flip at  wallenberg that was incredibly  impressive  but the footage that became even more  iconic is a compilation of him crashing  again and again so when somebody  in a video part you see them you know  failing something  five times ten times fifteen times  twenty times and then  finally getting it what matters is as  much  all the failed attempts as the final  thing  one of the biggest moments in  skateboarding history  was a fail it happened when jamie thomas  broke his board trying to land an  18-foot drop at a high school in  southern california  he didn't land it but the spot earned  the name the leap of faith  it's places where people have invested  blood sweat and tears  literally people can get extremely  impassioned about them  and feel a sense of loss when they go  and i think what skateboarding has  always been good at is  it tests our idea of what we want public  space to be  whether it's at the south banks in  london the ledges at the museum of  contemporary art in barcelona  the worst hour benches in berlin or a  25-foot stair set  in lyon skateboarders i think are a  constant reminder  that our cities can be creative and rich  [Music]  [Applause]  places  you

